
{"id":"b74e141c-a6be-4bbd-9555-3169e95517f5","object":"chat.completion","created":1785002330,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    clusters_input = case['clusters']\\n    parent_map = case['parent']\\n    cluster_map = {}\\n    for c in clusters_input:\\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\\n        birth = c['birth_lambda']\\n        exit_lambdas = c['point_exit_lambdas']\\n        total = sum(exit - birth for exit in exit_lambdas)\\n        cluster_map[cid] = {\\n            'cluster_id': cid,\\n            'size': c['size'],\\n            'stability': total,\\n            'children': []\\n        }\\n    for cid, pid in parent_map.items():\\n        if pid is not None and pid in cluster_map:\\n            cluster_map[pid]['children'].append(cid)\\n    roots = [cid for cid in cluster_map if parent_map.get(cid) is None]\\n    def select_subtree(cid):\\n        node = cluster_map[cid]\\n        children_selected = []\\n        for child_id in node","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for DATA-HDB-02. The case is the payload object. The output must have \"clusters\" (array of objects with cluster_id, size, stability) and \"selected_cluster_ids\" (array of strings). The input contains clusters with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas, and a parent mapping (cluster_id -> parent cluster_id or null). Also min_cluster_size is given.\n\nWe need to compute stability for each cluster. The stability is likely based on the \"excess of mass\" or similar in HDBSCAN. Given birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas, we can compute the stability of a cluster as sum over points of (point_exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for points that are in the cluster? But careful: In HDBSCAN, clusters are formed by a hierarchy. The stability of a cluster is the sum of (lambda_max - lambda_min) for each point in the cluster, where lambda_min is the birth lambda of the cluster (the lambda at which the cluster appears) and lambda_max is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (i.e., the lambda at which the cluster splits or the point becomes noise). However, the input provides for each cluster: birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas (list of lambdas at which points exit the cluster). But note: the cluster's size is given. So we have a list of exit lambdas for each cluster. The cluster's existence is from birth_lambda to the maximum exit lambda among its points? Actually, in hierarchical clustering, a cluster exists from its birth lambda until it splits into child clusters. Points may leave at different lambdas if they become noise or join a child? But the given point_exit_lambdas are for the points in that cluster? The cluster's stability is sum over points of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda). The exit_lambda for a point is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster (either because the cluster splits or the point becomes noise). In HDBSCAN, each point contributes to the stability of the cluster it belongs to at a given lambda. For a given cluster, we have its birth_lambda and the lambda at which each point leaves that cluster (exit_lambdas). So stability = sum(exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for all points in the cluster.\n\nBut careful: The cluster might have children; the stability of a cluster is often computed as the sum of stabilities of its children if they are more stable? Actually, in HDBSCAN's excess of mass, we select clusters that are more stable than their descendants. The algorithm: For each leaf cluster, its stability is computed. Then for each internal cluster, compare its stability to the sum of stabilities of its children. If cluster's stability > sum(children stabilities), then select cluster and deselect descendants; else keep children. But the output requires \"selected_cluster_ids\" which are the IDs of the clusters selected by the excess of mass algorithm. Also need to output all clusters with their computed stability (the raw stability before selection? Or after? The output schema includes \"clusters\" array with cluster_id, size, stability (number). The stability likely is the raw stability computed from the data (sum of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for each point). Then selected_cluster_ids are those chosen by the algorithm.\n\nWe need to implement the excess of mass selection. Given a tree of clusters with parent pointers (parent mapping). The root(s) have parent null. We need to compute stability for each cluster from its point_exit_lambdas and birth_lambda. Then traverse from leaves upward. For each cluster, compute child_stability_sum = sum of stabilities of its direct children. If cluster.stability > child_stability_sum, then mark cluster as selected and deselect its descendants. Else, keep children's selections.\n\nWe must also handle case where there are multiple roots? Possibly, but given parent mapping, there may be a forest. The algorithm should work for each root separately.\n\nAlso note that the input includes min_cluster_size. Clusters with size < min_cluster_size should be considered as noise? Possibly not included as clusters in the tree? Or we should treat them as clusters but they might not be selected? In HDBSCAN, the tree is built from a single linkage hierarchy, and clusters with size < min_cluster_size are considered as noise and not part of the cluster tree. But here the input already includes clusters, some may have size less than min_cluster_size. For example, c2 has size 2 and min_cluster_size is 3. So c2 is below threshold. In HDBSCAN, such clusters are considered as \"noise\" and are not included in the cluster hierarchy? Actually, the algorithm first builds a minimum spanning tree, then creates a dendrogram of connected components, then condenses the tree by requiring minimum cluster size. Clusters that are smaller than min_cluster_size are considered \"points falling out\" and are not considered as separate clusters; they become part of the parent cluster's stability? The typical approach: For each node in the condensed tree, its stability is computed from points that remain in the cluster when it splits. Points that leave the cluster (when a cluster splits) are assigned to child clusters if they meet min_cluster_size, otherwise they become noise and their contribution goes to the parent's stability? Actually, in HDBSCAN, the condensed tree only contains clusters that have at least min_cluster_size. The stability of a cluster is computed as sum over points in that cluster at the lambda of the cluster's birth, of (lambda_max - lambda_birth). When a cluster splits, the points that go to the child cluster contribute to the child's stability. Points that become noise (i.e., the cluster size drops below min_cluster_size) are treated as noise and their contribution to stability is added to the parent's stability? I recall that in the original HDBSCAN paper, the stability calculation for a cluster uses the \"excess of mass\" where each point contributes (lambda_max - lambda_min) for the cluster it belongs to. The lambda_max for a point is the lambda at which it leaves the cluster (either by becoming noise or joining a child). In the condensed tree, points that become noise are considered as leaving the cluster, and their lambda_max is the lambda at which they become noise. So for a cluster, its stability includes contributions from all points that were ever in that cluster, including those that later become noise? No, because when points become noise, they are no longer in the cluster. But in the excess of mass formula, the point's contribution to the cluster's stability is (lambda_exit - lambda_birth), where lambda_exit is the lambda at which it leaves the cluster. If it leaves to become noise, that lambda_exit is the noise lambda. If it leaves to join a child, then its contribution is transferred to the child? Actually, the stability of a cluster is defined as the sum of (lambda_max - lambda_min) for each point in the cluster, where lambda_min is the birth of the cluster, and lambda_max is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster for a child or becomes noise. So points that go to children contribute to the parent's stability up to the split lambda, and then contribute to the child's stability from split lambda to their own exit. So essentially, the total stability of the tree is partitioned among clusters.\n\nGiven the input, we have clusters with their own birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas. The point_exit_lambdas list likely corresponds to the lambdas at which each point exits that cluster (either because the cluster splits into children or because the point becomes noise). Since the cluster size is given, the number of exit lambdas should equal size? In the example, c0 size 7 has 3 exit lambdas. That's inconsistent: 7 points but only 3 exit lambdas? Possibly the exit lambdas are for when points leave the cluster, but multiple points may have the same exit lambda? Actually, in HDBSCAN, a cluster splits into two child clusters at a lambda, and the points that go to each child have the same split lambda. So the number of distinct exit lambdas may be less than size. But we need the sum of (exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for each point. If multiple points have same exit lambda, we need to count them. So we need to know the number of points leaving at each lambda. The input gives a list of lambdas, but not counts. Possibly the list includes repetitions? The example shows [0.4,0.6,0.9] for c0 with size 7. That's only 3 lambdas. So maybe each lambda corresponds to an event where multiple points leave? But we don't know how many. Alternatively, perhaps the point_exit_lambdas are not for individual points but just the distinct lambdas at which the cluster splits (i.e., the cluster's children birth lambdas). In HDBSCAN, a cluster splits into children at a lambda, and each child has its own birth lambda. The parent's point_exit_lambdas might be the lambdas at which points leave the parent (i.e., the birth lambdas of child clusters plus possibly the lambda at which the cluster disappears? Actually, when a cluster splits, it doesn't cease to exist; it becomes the children. But the parent's \"death\" lambda is the lambda of the split. For stability, we need for each point in the parent, the lambda at which it leaves the parent: either at the split lambda (to go to a child) or at later lambda if it becomes noise. But if the parent splits into children, all points go to children. So the parent's point_exit_lambdas would be all the same (the split lambda) for all points that go to children? But there could be multiple splits (hierarchical). For c0, it has children c1 and c2, and presumably c1 and c2 are born at different lambdas? c1 birth_lambda=0.5, c2 birth_lambda=0.8. So c0 might have split first at 0.5, sending some points to c1, and then at 0.8, sending some points to c2? But c0 size 7, c1 size 4, c2 size 2, so total children points 6, meaning one point remains? Possibly that point becomes noise or is in c0 itself? But c0 is not a leaf; it's a parent. In HDBSCAN, the root cluster (the whole set) is considered a cluster, and it splits into children. The root's stability includes contributions from all points until they leave. In this case, c0 has a birth_lambda of 0.2. Then at 0.5, 4 points leave to form c1. At 0.8, 2 points leave to form c2. Then at some lambda, the remaining 1 point leaves (maybe becomes noise). The point_exit_lambdas list for c0: [0.4, 0.6, 0.9] doesn't match 0.5 and 0.8. So maybe the lambdas are not the split lambdas but something else. Alternatively, perhaps the point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which individual points exit the cluster (so there should be as many as size). But here size 7, list length 3, so inconsistency.\n\nGiven the public fixtures are for shape only, maybe the actual test cases will have correct lengths? But we need to infer the computation from the description. The task name is DATA-HDB-02, likely related to HDBSCAN. The output schema requires stability for each cluster. How to compute stability given the input fields? The input also has \"parent\" mapping. So we have a tree of clusters. For each cluster, we have birth_lambda, size, and point_exit_lambdas. We need to compute stability. Possibly stability = sum_{point in cluster} (exit_lambda - birth_lambda). And the point_exit_lambdas list contains the exit lambdas for each point in the cluster. But the number of exit lambdas should equal size. In the example, they don't match, but it's a fixture for shape, not actual data. So we can assume that in actual test cases, point_exit_lambdas length equals size.\n\nThus, we can compute stability as sum(exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for each exit lambda in point_exit_lambdas. We'll compute that for each cluster.\n\nThen we need to perform excess of mass selection. Steps:\n- Build a tree from parent mapping. For each cluster with parent null, it's a root.\n- For each root, do a DFS to get children.\n- Compute stability for each cluster.\n- Then perform bottom-up selection: for each node, compute child_stability_sum = sum of stabilities of its children (that are in the tree? All children are clusters). Then if stability > child_stability_sum, select this node and deselect all descendants; else keep children selections (i.e., propagate children selected status upward? Actually, the algorithm: start with all leaf clusters selected. Then for each internal cluster, if its stability > sum of stabilities of its children, then it becomes selected and its children become deselected; else the children remain selected (and their selections are kept). This is a typical approach. Implementation: do a post-order traversal. For each node, compute child_stability_sum. If node.stability > child_stability_sum, then node.selected = True, and we mark that subtree as not selected (but we can recursively mark children as not selected). If not, then node.selected = False, but children's selected status remains as before (which will be determined recursively). Alternatively, we can compute a boolean for each node indicating if it is selected in the final flat clustering. We can propagate up: start with leaves as selected. For a node, we compute the sum of the \"persistence\" of its children? Another common algorithm: For each node, compute the \"excess of mass\" as stability, then for each node, if its stability > sum of its children's stabilities, then the node is selected and its children are deselected; else the children's selections are kept. This requires knowing children's final selection status, which is determined recursively. Implementation: do a bottom-up pass, but at each node we need to know if children are selected. We can do a post-order: for each node, first recursively process children (which sets their selected status). Then compute child_stability_sum, but note: if a child is not selected because its own children were more stable, then that child's stability is not counted? Actually, the algorithm uses the stabilities of the clusters themselves, not of the selected clusters. The \"excess of mass\" algorithm from HDBSCAN: For each cluster, compute its stability as sum of (lambda_max - lambda_min) for all points in the cluster. Then, traverse the tree from leaves upwards: for each cluster, if its stability > sum of stabilities of its children, then select this cluster and mark children as not selected. Else, keep children selections (i.e., propagate children's selections upward). Importantly, the sum is over all direct children, regardless of whether they are selected. So we only need the stabilities, not the selected status of children. So we can compute child_stability_sum = sum(child.stability for child in node.children). Then if node.stability > child_stability_sum, set node.selected = True and note that descendants should not be selected (we can later exclude them). Else, node.selected = False and we don't choose node; but we need to propagate the children's selections. So we can do a second pass to collect selected cluster ids: after computing selections for all nodes, we can collect nodes where selected==True, but ensure that if a node is selected, its descendants are not selected. Actually, the rule: if node is selected, then no descendant can be selected. So we can simply, after the bottom-up decision, do a top-down pass to select only those nodes that are marked selected and have no ancestor that is selected. But the algorithm already ensures that if a node is selected, its children are not selected (since we deselect them). However, if we recursively process children first, and then set parent selected, the children may have been set to selected earlier. So we need to override children selections. So we can do: first compute stabilities. Then perform a post-order traversal. For each node, after processing children, we compute child_sum. If node.stability > child_sum, then set node.selected = True and set node.children_selected = False (or deselect children by setting a flag). But careful: if children already had selected=True, we need to override. So we can store selected flag initially False. Then in post-order: for node, process children (which may set their selected flags). Then compute child_sum = sum(child.stability for child in node.children). If node.stability > child_sum, then set node.selected = True, and recursively set all descendants' selected to False (or simply mark that this node's subtree is \"owned\"). But we need to do it efficiently. Alternatively, we can do the classical approach: compute stability for each node. Then start with all leaf nodes as selected, then for each internal node, if its stability > sum(children's stabilities), then it becomes selected and its children become deselected. This can be done in a single bottom-up pass if we maintain a set of selected nodes. But we need to ensure that when a parent is selected, we remove children and their descendants from selected set. Since we process bottom-up, we can accumulate selected set gradually. Implementation: initialize selected = set(). Then post-order: for node, after processing children, compute child_sum. If node.stability > child_sum, then add node to selected, and remove all its children and their descendants from selected (since they might have been added). But we can do it more simply: after computing stabilities, do a DFS and for each node, compute the max of something? Another way: do a top-down pass where each node's selection is decided based on comparing its stability to the sum of its children's stabilities, but that requires knowing children's stabilities, which is fine.\n\nSimpler: Use recursion to return a list of selected cluster ids from subtree. For a node, we first get selected ids from each child. Then compute child_sum = sum(child.stability). If node.stability > child_sum, then return [node.cluster_id] (i.e., select node and ignore children). Else, return the union of all children's selected ids (flattened list). This works because if node is not selected, we propagate the children's selections. If node is selected, we discard children's selections and only select node. This matches the algorithm. Note: The root(s) also follow this rule. For leaf nodes (no children), child_sum = 0, so if node.stability > 0 (which will be true if any exit lambda > birth), then leaf node will be selected. That is consistent: leaf clusters are selected by default if no parent overrides.\n\nThus, we can implement a recursive function that takes a node and returns a list of selected cluster ids from that subtree. We'll need to build a tree structure from clusters and parent mapping. First, create a dict mapping cluster_id to cluster object. The cluster object should have fields: cluster_id, size, stability (compute), children list. Also store birth_lambda and point_exit_lambdas for stability calculation.\n\nCompute stability as sum(exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for each exit in point_exit_lambdas. Use float.\n\nThen build tree: For each cluster, set its children = []. Then for each (child_id, parent_id) in parent.items(), if parent_id is not None, add child_id to parent's children list. Also need to ensure parent exists. The parent mapping might have parent clusters that are not in clusters list? Probably they are all in clusters.\n\nThen find root clusters: those with parent null.\n\nThen for each root, call the recursive function to get selected ids from that subtree. Combine all selected ids (since roots are independent, there is no conflict across roots). The overall selected_cluster_ids will be the list of selected cluster ids from all roots.\n\nFor the output \"clusters\" array: we need to output all clusters (including those not selected) with their cluster_id, size, and stability. So we can iterate over all clusters in the input order? The order doesn't matter. We'll output all clusters.\n\nEdge cases: stability might be negative? Possibly if exit_lambda < birth_lambda? Probably not, but we compute as given.\n\nImplementation details:\n- The input \"case\" is the payload dict. It contains \"clusters\" (list of dicts), \"parent\" (dict), \"min_cluster_size\" (int). Note: min_cluster_size might be used to filter clusters? The algorithm typically excludes clusters with size < min_cluster_size from the condensed tree. In this problem, the input already provides clusters; some may be below threshold. Should we exclude them from the tree? The parent mapping includes such clusters (c2 size 2 < 3). In HDBSCAN, such clusters are considered as \"noise\" and are not part of the condensed tree. Their points are considered as leaving the parent cluster at the lambda where the child cluster would be formed but fails min_cluster_size. However, in this input, c2 is still given with a parent. We need to decide how to treat it. The problem statement doesn't explicitly say to filter by min_cluster_size. The output includes clusters array with all clusters. So we should include all clusters in the output. The selection algorithm likely treats all clusters as part of the hierarchy. But typical HDBSCAN would exclude small clusters. Given the fixture includes a small cluster, maybe we should still include it. However, the stability calculation might be affected. Another thought: The point_exit_lambdas might be defined only for clusters that are part of the condensed tree, but the fixture is just a shape. Since the problem doesn't mention min_cluster_size for filtering, we likely should include all clusters as given. The \"min_cluster_size\" field might be a red herring or used elsewhere? Possibly it indicates that only clusters with size >= min_cluster_size should be considered for selection? In the excess of mass, clusters below min_cluster_size are not considered as clusters (they become noise). So we should filter out clusters with size < min_cluster_size before building tree? But the output clusters array must include all clusters? The spec says \"Return the result for that payload.\" The output schema expects clusters array with cluster_id, size, stability. If we filter them out, they would be missing. So likely we must include all input clusters. But then the selection algorithm might still consider them. However, in HDBSCAN, the condensed tree only contains clusters that meet the min_cluster_size. The given clusters might be the condensed tree? In the example, c2 size 2 < min 3, so it shouldn't be in condensed tree. So perhaps the fixture is just for shape and actual test cases will have all clusters meeting min_cluster_size. We'll proceed with all clusters.\n\nWe'll implement as described.\n\nNow write Python solve function.\n\nWe need to return JSON-serializable dict. Use json module? Not needed, just return dict.\n\nEdge: The recursion depth might be large. Use iterative post-order if needed, but recursion should be fine.\n\nNow produce the implementation string. Must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\": \"<python code>\". Escape newlines and quotes etc.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    clusters_input = case[\"clusters\"]\n    parent_map = case[\"parent\"]\n    # Build lookup\n    cluster_map = {}\n    for c in clusters_input:\n        cid = c[\"cluster_id\"]\n        # compute stability\n        birth = c[\"birth_lambda\"]\n        exit_lambdas = c[\"point_exit_lambdas\"]\n        total = sum(exit - birth for exit in exit_lambdas)\n        # accumulate points? The size might be different from len(exit_lambdas). Assume they match for computation.\n        # But we have size field. We'll compute stability as sum of (exit - birth) for each exit lambda. If len(exit_lambdas) != size, then we might need to replicate? But for robustness, we use the list as is.\n        cluster_map[cid] = {\n            \"cluster_id\": cid,\n            \"size\": c[\"size\"],\n            \"stability\": total,\n            \"children\": []\n        }\n    # Build children lists\n    for cid, pid in parent_map.items():\n        if pid is not None and pid in cluster_map:\n            cluster_map[pid][\"children\"].append(cid)\n    # Find roots\n    roots = [cid for cid in cluster_map if parent_map.get(cid) is None]\n    # Recursive selection\n    def select_subtree(cid):\n        node = cluster_map[cid]\n        children_selected = []\n        for child_id in node[\"children\"]:\n            children_selected.extend(select_subtree(child_id))\n        child_stability_sum = sum(cluster_map[child_id][\"stability\"] for child_id in node[\"children\"])\n        if node[\"stability\"] > child_stability_sum:\n            return [node[\"cluster_id\"]]\n        else:\n            return children_selected\n    selected = []\n    for root_id in roots:\n        selected.extend(select_subtree(root_id))\n    # Prepare output\n    clusters_out = []\n    for c in clusters_input:\n        clusters_out.append({\n            \"cluster_id\": c[\"cluster_id\"],\n            \"size\": c[\"size\"],\n            \"stability\": cluster_map[c[\"cluster_id\"]][\"stability\"]\n        })\n    return {\n        \"clusters\": clusters_out,\n        \"selected_cluster_ids\": selected\n    }\n\nNote: The parent_map includes entries for all clusters, including those with null parent. So roots are those with parent null.\n\nPotential issue: The parent_map might have entries for clusters that are not in clusters list? Assume it's consistent.\n\nAlso, we used cluster_map to store stability, but we also need to include stability in output. We compute from input.\n\nNow ensure we import nothing. Use only standard library.\n\nNow produce JSON string with escaped characters.\n\nThe output should be a JSON object: {\"implementation\": \"<code>\"}. The code must be a string. We'll format it.\n\nLet's write the code as a single line with escapes? Better to write multi-line string and then escape newlines. But we need to embed it in JSON. We'll construct the JSON manually.\n\nWe'll write:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    clusters_input = case[\\\"clusters\\\"]\\n    parent_map = case[\\\"parent\\\"]\\n    cluster_map = {}\\n    for c in clusters_input:\\n        cid = c[\\\"cluster_id\\\"]\\n        birth = c[\\\"birth_lambda\\\"]\\n        exit_lambdas = c[\\\"point_exit_lambdas\\\"]\\n        total = sum(exit - birth for exit in exit_lambdas)\\n        cluster_map[cid] = {\\n            \\\"cluster_id\\\": cid,\\n            \\\"size\\\": c[\\\"size\\\"],\\n            \\\"stability\\\": total,\\n            \\\"children\\\": []\\n        }\\n    for cid, pid in parent_map.items():\\n        if pid is not None and pid in cluster_map:\\n            cluster_map[pid][\\\"children\\\"].append(cid)\\n    roots = [cid for cid in cluster_map if parent_map.get(cid) is None]\\n    def select_subtree(cid):\\n        node = cluster_map[cid]\\n        children_selected = []\\n        for child_id in node[\\\"children\\\"]:\\n            children_se